## The bus-off disagreement is SETTLED — and both parties were right about different faults [CANV2-BUSOFF-EXPERIMENT]

- the-disagreement: A CAN specialist held that a lone transmitter reaches bus-off after roughly 32 attempts. ISO 11898-1's error-passive ACK exception says it should stall at error-passive instead. The dispatch asked for the cheap experiment that settles it.
- experiment: MEASURED. Bridge stopped, `can1` brought DOWN to remove the ONLY ACK peer, `can0` brought up with `restart-ms 0` so any bus-off would be observable rather than silently auto-recovered, then ONE frame sent and the state sampled for 20 seconds.
- result: The interface went to ERROR-PASSIVE within 1 second and STAYED THERE for the full 20 seconds. Final counters: `error-warn 4`, `error-pass 5`, `bus-off 0`, `re-started 0`.
- verdict: ISO 11898-1's ACK exception is CONFIRMED. A node alone on a terminated segment with no peer to ACK does NOT reach bus-off. An error-passive transmitter that detects an acknowledgement error does not increment its transmit error counter, so TEC stalls near 128 and never reaches 256.
- but-the-specialist-is-also-right: INFERENCE, and it reconciles the two positions. The c3lcd board's own boot banner records that in NORMAL mode on its hardware it reaches BUS-OFF in about 5 seconds. The difference is the FAULT, not the standard: a node whose transceiver driver is dead sees BIT errors, and a bit error DOES increment TEC even when error-passive. The ACK exception applies only to the acknowledgement slot. So "lone node stalls at error-passive" and "faulty transmitter reaches bus-off in ~32 attempts" are both correct and describe different failures.
- consequence-for-restart-ms: This EXPLAINS the standing observation that `restart-ms 100` on the Pi has never fired. It has never fired because the Pi's dominant fault mode cannot produce bus-off. The instrument was not broken; the condition never occurred.
- part-of: [SPEC:CANV2-SWEEP]

