## FIRST MEASURED CROSS-SILICON v2 INTEROP — the Pi encoded it, the ESP32 decoded it [CANV2-INTEROP-PROOF]

- why-this-is-the-headline: [CXC-VISION-EPISTEMICS] `rule-loopback-proves-nothing-about-peers` says a node talking to itself proves the software and the adapter and NOTHING about interoperation, and that this is exactly how [BP-CAN-ID-DIVERGENCE] stayed invisible. Every prior v2 confirmation in this fleet was a loopback or a parity test against a baked corpus. This is the first time TWO DIFFERENT IMPLEMENTATIONS ON DIFFERENT SILICON agreed about a v2 frame on real wire.
- the-test: MEASURED. The c3lcd board subscribes `_device/broadcast/announce` (hash `0xC77C`). The bridge emits exactly one such frame at startup. The board's serial console was captured while the bridge was restarted.
- the-result: The board logged `BUS _device/broadcast/announce = 1`, then `announce: poll on _device/broadcast/announce — republishing the full set`. It received the frame, resolved the 16-bit hash to the correct namespace, decoded the value, and acted on it.
- what-this-proves: The Pi's Python v2 encoder and the ESP32's C++ v2 decoder agree on all of `prio`, `type`, `ns_id`, `src` and `scope` across a real differential pair. It also proves the ENTIRE receive chain end to end — Pi transceiver, cable, the C3 breakout's SN65HVD230 RECEIVER, the C3's TWAI RX path, and the namespace registry.
- what-it-refutes: `docs/board-bringup/esp32c3-supermini-can.md` records an open bench question, "CANH/CANL between the C3's SN65HVD230 and the Pi HAT appear to be open". That is now REFUTED FOR THE RECEIVE DIRECTION. The pair carries traffic. That document should be corrected.
- what-it-does-not-prove: NOTHING about TRANSMIT from the ESP32. The board is listen-only and is invisible on the wire by construction. See [CANV2-TRANSCEIVER].
- part-of: [SPEC:CANV2-SWEEP]

---

## The one remaining hardware fault is now isolated to a single component [CANV2-TRANSCEIVER]

- the-fault: DATASHEET-INDEPENDENT MEASUREMENT, recorded on the board and re-read this session from its boot banner. The SN65HVD230's DRIVER is dead — Pi bus-errors unmoved across ten 1.5 s dominant attempts, and −0.6 mV across CANH/CANL with the driver input held low, on TWO SEPARATE MODULES. Its RECEIVER is fine: 1,136 frames at 500 kbit/s, `bus_err=0`, verified twice.
- newly-narrowed-this-session: [CANV2-INTEROP-PROOF] removes the cabling hypothesis. The wire is good, the receiver is good, the TWAI RX path is good, and the v2 decode is good. The ONLY unproven link in the chain is the transceiver's driver output.
- briefing-claim-corrected: The dispatch said "CAN transmit from an ESP32 has NEVER worked in this fleet". That is too strong. `docs/board-bringup/esp32c3-supermini-can.md` records the C3 transmitting — `heartbeat 1 sent`, then `tx_err` climbing to bus-off. Frames LEAVE the TWAI controller. What never reached the segment is the differential output of the dead driver. The distinction matters because it says the fault is one component, not the silicon or the stack.
- what-david-must-decide: EMPTY — whether a REPLACEMENT transceiver has been fitted. This cannot be established from software; every instrument available to this session reads through the dead driver. NEEDS DAVID'S EYES.
- if-a-good-transceiver-is-fitted: The path is documented in the board's own banner and needs no reflash. Either reflash without `CXC_CAN_LISTEN_ONLY`, or take the two-hop bus route — publish `_device/c3lcd-047dd8/transport = "mqtt"` (5 bytes, fits a classic CAN frame), reboot onto MQTT, then publish `_device/c3lcd-047dd8/transport = "can listen=0"` over MQTT and reboot. `"can listen=0"` is 13 bytes and does not fit one classic CAN frame, which is why it takes two hops. Direct NVS also works: `can.listen = 0` in namespace `composeio`.
- the-payoff-if-it-works: This board's `ns_announce` is ALREADY ARMED — 6 names, 173 payload bytes, CRC `0x50AE`, on `_device/announce`. It currently logs `announcement ABANDONED at frame 0/29: the transport refused the header frame`. The moment transmit works the board self-announces and the hand-typed register list becomes unnecessary. See [CANV2-REGISTER-UNMADE].
- a-firmware-gap-worth-recording: The banner states that in normal mode this node reaches bus-off in about 5 s and then STOPS RECEIVING TOO, because ESP-IDF does not auto-recover and this driver never calls `twai_initiate_recovery()`. That is a real gap that will bite the moment the board transmits onto a segment with any fault. It is the ESP32 analogue of the `restart-ms` question and it has no counted-recovery instrument either.
- part-of: [SPEC:CANV2-SWEEP]

